WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:03.770
This series takes a closer look at each part of HASTE.

00:04.200 --> 00:05.690
In this bonus episode:

00:06.040 --> 00:08.340
the Open Data Catalog, where you can find

00:08.360 --> 00:10.560
free disaster imagery and add it

00:10.580 --> 00:12.550
to an image layer in a few clicks.

00:16.000 --> 00:18.300
We start a new image layer in the Spokane

00:18.320 --> 00:21.490
project and click Browse Open Data Catalog.

00:22.600 --> 00:26.560
After major disasters, Vantor and Planet release satellite

00:26.580 --> 00:29.770
imagery for free through their Open Data Programs.

00:30.700 --> 00:34.850
It lists recent events—wildfires, floods, earthquakes,

00:34.900 --> 00:37.630
and typhoons—with the provider next to each one.

00:38.507 --> 00:40.177
We pick the Spokane wildfire.

00:41.267 --> 00:43.677
Each scene's footprint appears on the map.

00:44.437 --> 00:46.187
Nine Vantor scenes for Spokane,

00:46.437 --> 00:49.047
two from before the fire, and seven from after.

00:50.100 --> 00:52.290
We filter to the post-event scenes.

00:54.267 --> 00:56.657
Selecting a scene shows its metadata,

00:56.867 --> 00:59.787
like sensor, resolution, cloud cover,

00:59.807 --> 01:01.767
and off-nadir angle, and streams a

01:01.787 --> 01:03.857
full-resolution preview onto the map.

01:05.897 --> 01:07.407
Zoomed into the burned neighborhoods,

01:07.697 --> 01:10.787
this August 3 GeoEye image is buried in smoke.

01:15.067 --> 01:18.457
The WorldView Legion image from August 4 is much clearer.

01:23.417 --> 01:26.307
Streets, homes, and burn scars are easy to make out.

01:27.467 --> 01:30.397
Each scene covers hundreds of square kilometers,

01:30.647 --> 01:32.497
but we only need these neighborhoods.

01:33.437 --> 01:36.807
Set clip area lets us drag a box around our area of interest,

01:37.077 --> 01:39.627
and HASTE clips the imagery to it during processing.

01:40.650 --> 01:43.960
The list now shows only scenes that overlap the box,

01:44.130 --> 01:46.260
and marks the ones that fully cover it.

01:47.767 --> 01:51.077
We add the August 4 scene as post-event imagery.

01:52.827 --> 01:56.377
Over on the Pre tab, the Add button says Pre-event instead.

01:57.227 --> 01:59.177
It always matches the scene's phase,

01:59.327 --> 02:02.417
so imagery can't end up on the wrong side of the event.

02:03.523 --> 02:05.513
Pre-event imagery is optional, though,

02:05.743 --> 02:08.153
so we'll keep this layer post-event only.

02:09.133 --> 02:12.653
Back in the form, the scene's URL, capture date,

02:12.673 --> 02:14.803
and source type are already filled in.

02:15.713 --> 02:18.663
HASTE also records each scene's source and license,

02:18.873 --> 02:21.643
so datasets you publish later credit the provider.

02:24.300 --> 02:27.510
We add our building footprints as usual and submit.

02:30.210 --> 02:32.760
HASTE downloads the scene and clips it to our box.

02:36.000 --> 02:38.270
A few minutes later, the layer is ready.

02:38.930 --> 02:41.210
We open it in the labeling tool, and the clipped

02:41.230 --> 02:43.940
Vantor imagery loads like any other layer.

02:47.170 --> 02:50.130
Up close, burned-out foundations sit right next to

02:50.150 --> 02:53.600
intact homes, some coated in red fire retardant.

02:54.390 --> 02:56.610
From here, the standard labeling workflow

02:56.630 --> 02:59.260
continues, just like in episode 1.

03:02.800 --> 03:06.170
HASTE is open source under the MIT license.

03:06.640 --> 03:09.750
Deploy it to Azure with a single azd up command,

03:09.840 --> 03:12.050
or run it locally with Docker Compose.

03:12.500 --> 03:15.430
Find it on GitHub at microsoft/haste.
